{
  "$comment": "When control may travel over the OPC UA connection (#134), for every combination of profile, channel security, server-certificate pinning and the two lab overrides — driven through BOTH runtimes by tests/unit/test_control_gate.py and packages/server-node/test/control-gate.test.mjs, which build a policy from `env` and assert every field below. Two hand-written copies of a safety rule are two rules. `control` is the gate (also `server_identity.control`, and the `control` field of every audit record); `offered` is whether write_opcua_nodes is listed; `refusal` is the contract error key a call to it is refused with (null: allowed); `startup` is the Tool policy line. The rule: control needs a verified server identity — a channel with a SecurityPolicy other than None AND a pinned OPCUA_SERVER_CERT. OPCUA_ALLOW_INSECURE_CONTROL opens an unsecured channel only; OPCUA_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_SERVER_CONTROL opens a secured-but-unverified one only; neither stands in for the other, because encryption and peer authentication are independent properties. The profile and allowlists apply on top. OPCUA_SERVER_CERT is never read here: whether a pin is *valid* is decided at connect time (an expired one refuses to connect), so a pin in configuration is what the policy trusts.",
  "tool": "write_opcua_nodes",
  "call": { "nodes": [{ "node_id": "ns=2;i=2", "value": 1 }] },
  "cases": [
    {"name": "observe over an unsecured channel, no override", "env": {"OPCUA_PROFILE": "observe", "OPCUA_SECURITY_POLICY": "None"}, "control": "blocked", "offered": false, "refusal": "toolDisabled", "server_identity": {"channel_secured": false, "server_authenticated": false, "authentication_method": "none", "control": "blocked"}, "startup": "profile=observe control=blocked server-identity=none"},
